%requires qore >= 2.0
%modern

%requires(reexport) DataProvider
%requires(reexport) ExaRestClient
%requires(reexport) RestSchemaActions
%requires OpenApi3
%requires RestClientIo

module ExaDataProvider {
    version = "1.0";
    desc = "user module providing a data provider API for Exa neural-search services";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
    init = sub () {
        DataProvider::registerFactory(new ExaDataProviderFactory());

        DataProviderActionCatalog::registerApp(<DataProviderAppInfo>{
            "name": ExaDataProvider::AppName,
            "presentation_source_locale": "en",
            "display_name": "Exa",
            "short_desc": "Exa neural web search, research agents, batches and search monitors",
            "desc": "Provides access to [Exa](https://exa.ai) (formerly Metaphor) — a search API "
                "built for programs rather than for people: the query is meaning, not keywords, and "
                "the result can be the answer itself rather than a list of links.\n\n"
                "**What it covers**:\n"
                "- **Search** the web semantically, with the page contents returned alongside the "
                "results\n"
                "- **Answer a question** from the web, with the sources cited\n"
                "- **Research runs**: an agent that searches, reads and reasons until it can answer, "
                "returning prose or data shaped by a JSON schema you supply\n"
                "- **Batches**: run a large workload asynchronously in one job rather than one "
                "request per item\n"
                "- **Search monitors**: re-run a search on a schedule and have only what is new "
                "delivered to a webhook, de-duplicated — a query becomes a feed\n\n"
                "**Where this earns its place**: keyword search cannot express \"companies like "
                "this one\" or \"papers arguing the opposite\", and a team that needs an answer "
                "rather than ten tabs ends up writing the reading step itself.  Exa does both, and "
                "the monitors turn the same query into an ongoing feed without a polling loop or a "
                "de-duplication table to maintain.",
            "scheme": "exa",
            "logo": ExaLogo,
            "logo_file_name": "exa-logo.svg",
            "logo_mime_type": MimeTypeSvg,
            "groups": (DataProvider::AppGroup::AiLlm, DataProvider::AppGroup::WebAutomation,),
        });

        # every action generated from the vendored Exa Public API description; see ExaManifest for
        # what is exported and why
        map DataProviderActionCatalog::registerAction($1),
            ExaSchema::getActionSet().getActionInfoList(ExaDataProvider::AppName);
    };
}

/** @mainpage ExaDataProvider Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section exadataproviderintro ExaDataProvider Introduction

    Data-provider integration for <a href="https://exa.ai">Exa</a> (formerly Metaphor), a web-search API built
    for programs: the query is meaning rather than keywords, and the result can be the answer itself
    rather than a list of links.

    The application covers the Exa Public API:

    |!Group|!What it does
    |Search|search the web semantically, optionally returning the contents of each page
    |Answers|answer a question from the web, citing the pages used
    |Contents|fetch the text, the matching passages or a written summary of pages Exa knows
    |Research Agent|start, read, stop and delete runs of an agent that searches and reasons until it can answer
    |Batches|enqueue many requests to run asynchronously in one job and download the results
    |Search Monitors|re-run a search on a schedule and deliver only what is new to a webhook

    @section exadataproviderpicking Which Action to Use

    The four read actions overlap, and the difference is who does the reading:

    |!Action|!Returns|!Use it when
    |\c search|links, optionally with page text|the caller ranks or reads the results itself
    |\c answer|an answer with its sources|one question, answerable from a handful of pages
    |\c create-agent-run|a run to collect later|the answer needs several searches or many pages
    |\c contents|the text of pages already known|the URLs or document IDs are already in hand

    \c search and \c answer both take an \c outputSchema, which turns the result from prose into
    data shaped the way the calling flow already expects.

    @section exadataproviderusage Answering a Question

    @code{.py}
AbstractDataProvider exa = conn.getDataProvider();

# one question, answered from the web with its sources
hash<auto> answer = exa.getChildProviderEx("answer").doRequest({
    "query": "which open-source vector databases support hybrid search?",
    "model": "exa-pro",
});
printf("%s\n", answer.answer);
map printf("  %s\n", $1.url), answer.citations;

# a question that needs real research runs asynchronously
hash<auto> run = exa.getChildProviderEx("create-agent-run").doRequest({
    "instructions": "compare the licensing terms of the three largest commercial alternatives",
    "effort": "medium",
});
hash<auto> result = exa.getChildProviderEx("get-agent-run").doRequest({"id": run.id});
    @endcode

    @section exadataprovidermonitors Turning a Query Into a Feed

    A search monitor is a search Exa re-runs on a schedule, delivering what is new to a webhook.  Exa
    filters by date since the last run and removes near-duplicates, so the receiver gets new results
    rather than the same page every cycle — which is the part a caller would otherwise have to build
    and maintain itself.

    \c batch-monitors applies one action to every monitor matching a filter; a filter is required, so
    a mistyped request cannot act on the whole account, and \c dry_run defaults to \c True so the
    affected set can be read before anything changes.

    @section exadataproviderschema How the Actions Are Generated

    Every action is generated from the API description vendored with this module through
    @ref restschemaactionsintro "RestSchemaActions", so its options and result types are the vendor's
    own and an upstream change cannot alter the contract of an action a workflow already calls; see
    @ref ExaDataProvider::ExaManifest "ExaManifest" for what is exported and why, and
    @ref ExaDataProvider::ExaSchema "ExaSchema" for the pinned document's provenance.

    Exa writes its nullable enums as \c {anyOf: [enum, null]}, which loses the values in generation;
    the manifest restores them with display names, so the search type, category, live-crawl mode,
    answer model and agent effort are all dropdowns rather than free text.

    For agent-tool wiring see the @ref exarestclientintro "ExaRestClient" module's \c ExaAgentTools
    namespace.

    @section ExaDataProviderRelnotes Release Notes

    @subsection ExaDataProviderv1_0 ExaDataProvider v1.0
    - initial release
*/

#! contains all public definitions in the ExaDataProvider module
public namespace ExaDataProvider {
#! Exa application name
public const AppName = "Exa";
}

#! App logo loaded from file
public const ExaLogo = File::readTextFile(get_script_dir() + "/exa-logo.svg");
